welcome back to our course machine  learning for high-throughput biomedical  data in this lecture we will talk about  processing high throughput data  specifically we will take one of the  data sets from the publication we  started to review and prepared for  analysis in order to understand the  samples and where they came from we will  learn about breast cancer classification  and explore The Associated NCBI file  project but before we start it is  important to note that in the context of  machine learning data selection and  preparation are critical for good  analysis in fact many data scientists  and bioinformaticians spend the majority  of their time cleaning understanding and  preparing the data even before they get  started there is even a common saying  garbage in garbage out it means that  whether your model or analysis are good  or bad  if the source data is bad there is  nothing not even the advanced methods  and computational capabilities that will  make your results any good with that in  mind let's review the data we will be  working with in this lecture we will be  using the tion for platform well use it  to process raw data so make sure you  have the credentials you received with  the course if you don't have your  credentials or have trouble signing in  please email me directly the section we  will use is under NGS next generation  sequencing data it is called RNA seek  slash chip parallel analysis of NGS and  microarray data when using the platform  make sure that you take notice of the  informational pop ups that will appear  while building the pipeline these pop  ups provide detailed information on each  step that we take if you would like to  learn about the algorithm you can  usually find a link to a reference  publication in those pop ups so let's  review the data set we are dealing with  the main topic that it pursues is  precision medicine this time we will  talk about breast cancer and some of the  treatments available for this disease as  we saw in the previous lecture  high-throughput data like genomics  transcriptomics and others are becoming  more and more affordable and accessible  while precision medicine relies on such  information to diagnose patients to  understand disease at the molecular  level and even prescribe treatments to  patients relying on such information  let's take  look at what that exactly means as you  remember the publication with selected  deals with breast cancer a mammogram is  an x-ray picture of the breast that  doctors used to look for early signs of  breast cancer in this example you can  see that the tumor is highlighted once  it's found it is critical to establish  whether it's malignant or benign and  what tissue it is originating from once  the tumor is characterized by its  phenotype like size tissue origin etc  additional information can be obtained  by checking for presence of specific  proteins characteristic of cancer if  these proteins are known  immunohistochemistry or antibody  staining can be used to find if they are  expressed or not specific molecular  markers are characteristic of cellular  events such as proliferation or cell  death these are typically referred to as  biomarkers based on these biomarkers we  can know something about the tumor and  in many cases have a pretty good idea  about treatment options but a protein  expression especially measured with  immunohistochemistry provides us with  only a select number of biomarkers that  we can check for what if other processes  are going on that we should take into  consideration oftentimes  immunohistochemistry is used in  combination with gene expression panels  to explore such variation here on the  right you can see the Pam 50 panel it is  a panel of 50 genes that shows their  expression high expression is shown in  green and low and red now you can also  notice that we can see patterns or  profiles of gene expression using  various combinations of genes  researchers started classifying breast  cancer into several subtypes luminal a  normal like luminal B her - enriched and  basal like how can this molecular  subtyping be used because many patients  characterized with such methods were  observed for a long period of time we  can assess the risk levels for  recurrence and survival in this chart  you can see a Meyer Kaplan curve of  survival or recurrence based on to  clinical gene panel Pam 50 and Oncotype  DX these panels separate patients into  three groups  high medium and low risk based on these  assessments a treatment is selected  traditional treatments for cancer are  surgery radiotherapy and chemotherapy  each one of these treatments can have a  harmful side effect and is known to be  effective in very specific situations  for example tumors are typically  irradiated before surgery  or if they're considered to be somewhat  benign chemotherapy target cell  proliferation and often it ends up  affecting many other cells in the body  not just the tumor causing severe  discomfort to patients and in some cases  leading to complications however since  the 1990s a new type of treatment  started emerging in clinical use  targeted cancer therapy one such  treatment is called Herceptin or trust  to Zermatt it is a monoclonal antibody  that blocks the her2 receptor blocking  it from receiving the growth factor  hormone and preventing cell growth but  even though Herceptin is considered a  major breakthrough for patients with  her2 protein overexpression targeted  therapies have not become a silver  bullet cure for cancer in early stage  curable her2 positive breast cancer  trust is a map containing regimens  improved overall survival and  disease-free survival however the  studies also showed an increased risk of  heart disease and resistance developed  by most patients here you can see the  proposed mechanism of action for this  drug as you can see on the picture the  antibody causes a sequence of events  that disrupt the signaling and  proliferation cellular pathway on the  other side we see the disruption that  takes place these changes cause  resistance or lack of response mutations  polymorphisms and lack of expression are  all factors contributing to this  resistance the study of how Herceptin  works in its molecular mechanism of  action continues providing new insight  into combinations of factors that could  lead to a significant number of  decisions that should be taken when  prescribing the drug from observed  changes in receptor gene variation erbb2  encodes for the her2 receptor changes to  other players in this  pathways and other variation caused by  the treatment response this is a great  example of how the complexity of sub  cellular mechanisms lead to both  breakthrough therapies and new  challenges where each new challenge is  characterized by many players and  interactions between them  the study of these changes will be the  focus of this project where we'll take  the gene expression data and apply  machine learning to help us extract  meaningful insight into the nature of  pres cancer itself the publication we  are taking data from has over 50  different breast cancer subtypes so  approximately 10,000 genes are expressed  for each cell line that has an  associated clinical subtype the same  cell line were also treated with 70  different approved in experimental  compounds in IG 50 or inhibition of  growth profile for each cell line was  measured the first thing that we would  want to do is find this project on NCBI  so let's go ahead and do that on NCBI  data is organized in bio projects that  contain links to publications various  metadata about samples and if fraud data  is available it can be downloaded here's  how you find the data set and download  information about the samples let's take  a look at the download a table and see  what's inside  data is stored in a compressed format or  SRA sequence read archive the SRA  archive contains runs or samples they  start with the letters s RR or a  sequence read run if you download and  extract the SRA archives you will find  that they contain fast q files inside  with extensions underscore 1 and  underscore 2 at the end looking at the  metadata given with this project we can  see that the data was produced using  pair and sequencing it is a technique  that uses simultaneous reading of each  fragment from both ends thus improving  the quality of sequencing in addition to  the file name in its download location  the metadata shows cell line names used  in each run and the clinical subtype  associated with that sample to save us  some time  we already downloaded and on  the archive preparing it for us to use  on the platform so for our first  pipeline we will select some normal like  and cloud and low samples to do the  analysis we have five normal like and  six cloud and low samples you can do  your own search on the internet and read  more about these cell lines the cells  even look differently as you can see in  the picture what we also know about the  clinical classification is that cloud  and low is a much more aggressive cancer  that is often grouped under triple  negative breast cancer triple negative  means the tissue tests negative for  three main receptors ER or estrogen  receptor PR progesterone receptor and  her2/neu anapa dermal growth factor  receptor 2 I think that we now know what  kind of data we will be dealing with as  I said in the beginning we have to  carefully prepare the data for any  machine learning in the next session we  will take the uploaded files on the T  bio info server and run an RNA seek  pipeline to generate a table of  expression

